<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mubariz Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1724">1724</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mubariz Khan was a Mughal noble and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> who was appointed by the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> to oppose the growing independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Nizam-ul-Mulk</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. He was defeated and killed by Nizam-ul-Mulk at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shakar-kheda">Battle of Shakar Kheda</a> in October 1724, a victory that founded Hyderabad State.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mubariz Khan was born Khwaja Mohammad in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> as a Turani officer. Of a relatively obscure background, he entered Mughal service as a clerk; his career rose after he married the daughter of Inayat Khan Kashmiri, a noble in Aurangzeb's favour. He was bakshi of Prince Kam Baksh and faujdar of Sangamner, and in 1703-1704 received the title Amanat Khan as faujdar of Baizapur. Under Bahadur Shah he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, under Jahandar Shah he was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> as Shahamat Khan, and in 1713, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a>, he became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> with the title Mubariz Khan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He received wider powers than his predecessors in a province that had lost its splendour after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s death, suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> raids to extract <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a> and saw its trade decline. In the early months of 1714 he defeated a Maratha raiding party near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. His methods were violent: after a 40-day siege of the fort of Changapettah, held by the Telugu Nayak Venkat, he raised a tower of skulls, renamed the fort Farrukhnagar and sent Venkat's head to the emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><p>He sided with Hussain Ali Khan Barha, the viceroy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, rather than the emperor, and in December 1715 was confirmed as governor and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a>, which removed the central Mughal regime from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>'s finances. He then punished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> slow to pay their taxes, led a nine-month circuit through the country of the Godavari and, after a five-month siege of the fort of Cankoll, in which gunners sent by the English factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Masulipatnam</a> manned his guns, controlled the whole Andhra coast. The Maasir-ul-Umara praised him for driving off large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> armies with no more than 3,000 horse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1722 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Nizam-ul-Mulk</a>, Asaf Jah I, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a>, found large arrears in the appointments of Mubariz Khan and his sons. When the Nizam left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, Emperor Muhammad Shah issued a farman granting Mubariz Khan the governorship of the six provinces of the Deccan. Busy with a siege, Mubariz Khan was slow to respond and camped at Shakar Kheda while the Nizam, allied with Bajirao I, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> first. He died fighting the Nizam on 11 October 1724 and was buried the next day with all his followers. The historian John F. Richards describes him as a proto-dynastic figure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mubariz Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1724 he left the capital without permission, and when the governor favoured by the court, <b>Mubariz Khan</b>, opposed him, Nizam-ul-Mulk defeated and killed him at the Battle of Shakar Kheda in October 1724.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
